    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Ibn Sina</a> set out the medical knowledge of his time in five ordered books, fitting the traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates">Hippocrates</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s natural philosophy.</li><li>Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation entered European scholastic medicine, and university courses used the text from the 14th century onwards.</li><li>It lost ground in the 16th century as humanists preferred Greek and Roman authorities to Arabic ones, but it was still taught as background literature in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> in 1715.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Canon gives seven rules for experimenting with new drugs, taken partly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a>, among them that a drug should be tested on a single condition rather than a composite one and that experiments should be carried out on the human body.</p></aside>
    </section>

