    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1025</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Ibn Sina</a> completes the Canon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1052</span><span class="rf-text">The date of the earliest known copy of volume 5, now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aga-khan">Aga Khan</a> Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">12th century</span><span class="rf-text">The Canon is translated from Arabic into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">13th century</span><span class="rf-text">Nathan ha-Meati translates the Canon into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, and medical scholars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> begin to use it</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1472</span><span class="rf-text">The earliest printed edition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Canon appears, covering only book three</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1608</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> editions, fourteen of them in the 16th century, continue until this year</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1715</span><span class="rf-text">The Canon is still taught as background literature at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/galen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galen.jpg" alt="Galen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galenus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Georg Paul Busch (engraver), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The medical traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a>, and through him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates">Hippocrates</a>, had dominated Islamic medicine from its beginnings. The Canon presents an overview of the medical knowledge of the Islamic world, which had also been influenced by Persian, Chinese and Indian medicine. Galen's own medical writings ran to twenty volumes, and a more concise reference was wanted in contrast to them.</p><p>Its author, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Ibn Sina</a>, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> as Avicenna, sought to fit these inherited traditions into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s natural philosophy. The result is described as a clear and ordered summa of all the medical knowledge of his time. The title in Arabic is al-Qanun fi l-tibb. The word qanun, often translated as law or legal code, here suggests an ordered system or a complete, universal encyclopedia. The common medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> title was Canon Medicinae or Liber Canonis, from which the English title comes.</p><p>Ibn Sina defined medicine (tibb) as the science by which we learn the various states of the body, in health and when not in health, the means by which health is likely to be lost and, when lost, is likely to be restored. He held that the body cannot be restored to health unless the causes of both health and disease are determined.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Canon is divided into five books. Book 1 gives essays on basic medical and physiological principles, anatomy, regimen and general therapeutic procedures. Book 2 lists medical substances alphabetically after an essay on their general properties. Book 3 treats the diagnosis and treatment of diseases specific to one part of the body, and Book 4 conditions that cover several parts or the whole body. Book 5 is a formulary of compound remedies. Books 1, 3 and 4 are further divided into parts, chapters, subchapters, sections and subsections.</p><p>Book 1 holds six theses on the cosmic elements, the temperaments, the humours, anatomy and physiology, and explains the causes of health and disease. Book 2 lists about 800 simple floral, mineral and animal substances, each with its criteria of goodness, its nature, one or more of 22 possible general actions and its substitutes. Book 3 runs from head to foot through the organs and their diseases. Book 4 gives long sections to fevers, divided into ephemeral, putrid and hectic kinds, and ends with a treatise on personal hygiene. Book 5 lists 650 compound drugs, attributed to Arabic, Indian and Greek sources.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Ibn Sina</a> began writing the Canon in Gorganj, continued in Rey and completed it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> in 1025. He built it on the Four Humours of Hippocratic medicine but refined them. In explaining how disease arises he added his own view of different types of spirits, or vital life essences, and souls, whose disturbances might lead to bodily diseases through their close association with master organs such as the brain and heart.</p><p>In his account of the body he set out four causes of illness taken from Aristotelian philosophy: material, efficient, formal and final. He took the Aristotelian view that the heart is the ultimate source of the body's faculties, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a>'s view that the brain is the chief seat of sentient life, while allowing that the brain is where the rational faculty shows itself. In Book 5 he commented on differences between recipes from different sources, sometimes gave his own, and favoured remedies proven by experience, cautioning that compounds could have unexpected or much stronger effects than their separate components.</p><p>The thesis on the elements, which Oskar Cameron Gruner calls the foundation of the whole Canon, insists that a physician must assume the four elements of natural philosophy. In another work, the Treatise on Pulse, Ibn Sina combined his own view of spirits with that of the Four Humours to form a new doctrine of how various diseases arise.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristotle.jpg" alt="Aristotle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">After Lysippos, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Canon remained a medical authority for centuries and set the standards for medicine in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the Islamic world. Its encyclopaedic content, systematic arrangement and combination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a>'s medicine with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s science and philosophy helped it enter European scholastic medicine. Medical scholars started to use it in the 13th century, and university courses implemented the text from the 14th century onwards.</p><p>Its influence declined in the 16th century because humanists preferred ancient Greek and Roman authorities in medicine to Arabic ones, although others defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a>'s innovations beyond the original classical texts. It fell out of favour in university syllabi but was still being taught as background literature as late as 1715 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>. It is also an important text in Unani medicine, a form of traditional medicine practised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. William Osler described it as the most famous medical textbook ever written and said it remained a medical bible for a longer time than any other work. George Sarton, in his Introduction to the History of Science, called it an immense encyclopedia of medicine.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;The Canon of Medicine&#8221; cites where it covers Legacy and reception:</p><ul><li>Nancy Siraisi, Avicenna in Renaissance Italy : the Canon and medical teaching in Italian universities after 1500 (Princeton University Press, 1987)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gerard-of-cremona"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gerard-of-cremona.jpg" alt="Gerard of Cremona" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gerard-of-cremona">Gerard of Cremona</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-RaziInGerardusCremonensis1250.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gerard of Cremona, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The earliest known copy of volume 5, dated 1052, is held in the collection of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aga-khan">Aga Khan</a> and housed in the Aga Khan Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a>. The earliest printed edition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Canon appeared in 1472 and covered only book three. Soon afterwards eleven complete incunables were published, followed by fourteen more Latin editions in the 16th century, the last in 1608.</p><p>The Canon was translated from Arabic into Latin as Canon medicinae by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerard-of-cremona">Gerard of Cremona</a>; there appear to have been two men of that name, both translators of Arabic texts into Latin. Besides Latin, it was translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> by Nathan ha-Meati during the 13th century, and complete translations were made into Turkish and Persian during the 18th century. Gruner's English edition of the first book, a treatise on the Canon with a translation of Book 1, was published in 1973, and an English version of volume 1 appeared in 1999.</p><p>Its place in European teaching ended gradually. It fell out of favour in university syllabi in the 16th century, though it was still being taught as background literature as late as 1715 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>. In the Islamic world and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> it remained an important text in Unani medicine.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Canon was used as a standard medical textbook through the 18th century in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation greatly influenced the development of medieval medicine. Its rules for testing new drugs, its description of strokes, its long chapter on the nervous system and its treatment of fevers have all been studied by later readers.</p><p>Modern authors have examined particular passages. The information in Book 3 is described as representing some of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a>'s most important contributions to several fields, including atherosclerosis, pulsology, migraines, cataracts, vasovagal syncope and neuroscience. In its account of stroke, based on humoral medicine, the descriptions are still similar to the modern understanding of the condition. Book 3 lists fifteen kinds of headache and names conditions from epilepsy and paralysis to melancholia and insomnia, with treatments in three steps: a change of lifestyle, simple medicines and compound medicines, with surgery or other non-drug methods in some cases. Sarton singled out its thoughts on the contagious nature of phthisis and its descriptions of skin and nervous ailments.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;The Canon of Medicine&#8221; cites where it covers Legacy and reception:</p><ul><li>Nancy Siraisi, Avicenna in Renaissance Italy : the Canon and medical teaching in Italian universities after 1500 (Princeton University Press, 1987)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Canon of Medicine is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Ibn Sina</a>'s five-book encyclopedia of medicine, completed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> in 1025. Translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, it entered European scholastic medicine and served as a standard textbook in universities into the early modern period.</em></p>

